  "notes": "New-England folk-pop with a shouted communal chorus, straight 2&4 with stomp-clap substitution. The drum block is the entry's: \"kick and stomp on the beat, handclaps and tambourine on 2&4, brushed snare; drums enter at the second verse and the last chorus is the only full-kit section.\" So the kick sits on all four beats at near-zero syncopation — the stomp is on the beat, not around it — the snare is a soft brushed transient under a clap layer, the perc lanes are `tambourine` and `stomp`-shaped `tomLow` at `downbeat`, `hihat.continuous` is false at `fillDensity` 0.08 and the roll vocabulary is off. The late entry and single full-kit section are the arrangement's deep `dropOutBeats`. No `bass808` block: \"no 808. Upright or electric bass on roots, entering with the drums\", which is a kick mirror at 0.95 with a low `anticipationProb`. Harmony is \"guitar-friendly majors with heavy suspended and add9 voicings and a borrowed minor iv\", so `susSubstitutionProb` is 0.46, `ninth` is raised and the borrowed iv is the `borrowed` map. The melody block encodes the entry's lyric-first claim — the songs are built around delivery, \"wordy and syllable-dense in verses … and open into a wide-interval gang-sung chorus\" — as a `densityPerBar` running 5—14 with an arch contour and a raised `octaveJumpProb` for the chorus. The counter is the fiddle line the entry names. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says \"instrumentation and tempo bands partially inferred\": every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the harmonic weights, the melody register and `bpm.mode` 120 (the midpoint of the stated 110—130 core) are INFERRED. ⚠ The entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the banjo/mandolin/fiddle instrumentation claim is repeated here, and it is the reason the counter style is a reading rather than a credit.",
